From d484ca31d51af5d4507f750d1d5c88876ad41725 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: rautshivamxyz-beep <rautshivamxyz@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 03:48:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] v9.33.146 "A timer cannot crash the app"

The "timer 10 minutes" phone command parsed its own duration three lines
into a chat turn, and both of its arithmetic lines were reachable from
ordinary typed text:

  val n = m.groupValues[1].toInt()      // NumberFormatException
  'h' -> n * 3600                       // Int overflow, negative length

Nothing on the path caught either. tryPhoneCommand is called straight
from ncieSend with no try/catch around it, so "timer 99999999999999
minutes" threw NumberFormatException out of a chat message and took the
whole app down - one typed sentence, no model involved. "timer 1000000
hours" is the quieter one: 1000000 * 3600 wrapped Int to a negative
number and the clock app was handed a nonsense length.

The duration is now parsed by NcieTimer, a pure function with no Android
imports: the digits are read as a Long, the range is checked BEFORE any
multiplication (every unit multiplies by at least 1, so a count already
above the limit can never come back under it), and a digit run that does
not even fit a Long is answered the same way as any other over-long
timer. Nothing in the parser can throw.

The limit is 24 hours - a kitchen timer, not a calendar - and an
over-long request is refused in words rather than silently clamped to a
number the user never asked for. The confirmation toast now states the
duration NOVA actually handed to the clock app ("Timer set: 1 hour 30
minutes") instead of echoing the user's own text back.

The same pattern had a third failure: the integer-only regex read
"timer 1.5 hours" as the "5 hours" inside it and set FIVE hours - four
and a half hours too long, with a toast that agreed with the wrong
number. A fractional duration is now read as a fraction (a comma works
as the decimal mark too), in integer arithmetic so no float rounding
enters the seconds value.

TimerSpecTest pins all three by name - the Int-overflowing digit run,
the Int-overflowing hour count, and the fractional duration - plus a
hostile-input sweep asserting the parser never throws. verify_patches
gains eleven checks, including one that fails if the crashing inline
expression ever comes back, and the version floor moves to 9.33.146
(314).

No inference setting, model weight, permission or network path changed:
this is a phone-command parser, and it is fully offline.
